{"step_index":792,"source":"SYSTEM_SDK","type":"EPHEMERAL_MESSAGE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-19T11:19:48Z","content":"# Work state: your to-do lists and working state\nUse claude-mem's work_state_write tool to track all to-do lists and multi-step work. It is your canonical to-do list: use it instead of any built-in to-do tool. Also use it to track the state of anything you need an ongoing understanding of. Whatever is still open is shown here at the start of every session in this project.\n- One list per to-do list or tracked thing: work_state_write with list=\"<name>\" and the fields to set\n- To-do item: fields {\"task\": \"<name>\", \"status\": \"todo\" | \"doing\" | \"done\" | \"dropped\", ...any details}\n- State: fields {\"<key>\": <value>} (the latest value of each key wins; null clears a key; \"status\": \"done\" closes the list)\n- Read every list, closed items included: work_state_read with includeClosed=true\n\nNothing open yet.\n\n# claude-mem status\n\nThis project has no memory yet. The current session will seed it; subsequent sessions will receive auto-injected context for relevant past work.\n\nMemory injection starts on your second session in a project.\n\n`/learn-codebase` is available if the user wants to front-load the entire repo into memory in a single pass (~5 minutes on a typical repo, optional). Otherwise memory builds passively as work happens.\n\nLive activity: http://localhost:37701\n✨ 30 Day Free Trial — memory runs off-plan. Get up to 100% more usage from your plan https://cmem.ai/pro?from=welcome-hint\nHow it works: `/how-it-works`\n\nThis message disappears once the first observation lands.\n\n# claude-mem memory use\nModel-facing tool replies must be concise purpose-specific text; keep structured envelopes and internal metadata out of model context. Select only evidence useful to the current question, and expand from index to context to relevant details only as needed.\nUse claude-mem for your own durable notes. For local-worker notes, when its save_memory tool is available, save_memory({text, title, project}) records preferences, corrections, decisions, and reusable lessons. For server notes, use observation_add for the selected server project when available; save_memory is local-worker only. Search first with mem_search({query, mode: \"auto\", project}) to avoid duplicate or conflicting notes. For guided research, use mode: \"guided\" and follow each returned continuation through mem-search steps 1, 2, and 3. A hosted read-only connector may expose searches without write tools; do not call a missing write tool.\nUse work_state_write for to-do lists and changing work state when available. Keep durable notes concise, distinguish verified facts from guesses, and exclude credentials and private material the user asked you not to remember. Native memory files can remain enabled; an explicitly configured memory folder watcher imports their Markdown notes into claude-mem. Never assume a hosted memory search was intercepted: call mem_search directly when its result is needed."}
